                <pb facs="0835" n="737" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg737"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.003/614</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Crane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-23"><hi rendition="#italic">December 23,
                        1920</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received February 2, 1921.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 571</seg></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>With reference to the note of the Foreign Office, dated June 12th, 1920, on the
                    subject of the validity of inward transit passes, it is understood that an
                    identic note was addressed by the Foreign Office to other Legations; that the
                    French Legation has refused its consent on the ground of brigandage and
                    disturbed conditions; that the Japanese and Italian Legations have refused on
                    the ground that transit passes, being evidence of payment of dues, are not
                    subject to a time limit, while the British Legation has refused on the same
                    grounds as the American Legation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles R. Crane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
